1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,329
Every IT team knows this situation.

2
00:00:03,609 --> 00:00:08,017
The rule is written in one document, the coding standard in another,

3
00:00:08,017 --> 00:00:13,138
the test finding is sitting in a spreadsheet, and the outage report is in Word.

4
00:00:13,588 --> 00:00:16,148
Each one knows a part of the story.

5
00:00:16,428 --> 00:00:20,247
Nobody sees the full picture, until something breaks.

6
00:00:21,542 --> 00:00:27,847
So let's start small, with just one file: our non-functional requirements spreadsheet.

7
00:00:28,297 --> 00:00:31,832
As soon as it's uploaded, it is scanned for viruses,

8
00:00:31,832 --> 00:00:35,163
and the platform keeps a fingerprint of the file.

9
00:00:35,613 --> 00:00:38,553
So far, nothing leaves the server.

10
00:00:39,833 --> 00:00:44,232
Next, MarkItDown converts the spreadsheet into Markdown.

11
00:00:44,512 --> 00:00:49,051
Each sheet becomes a heading, and each row becomes a line in a table.

12
00:00:49,501 --> 00:00:52,623
See this row: no unbounded queries.

13
00:00:52,903 --> 00:00:56,959
Every list API must cap its page size on the server.

14
00:00:57,239 --> 00:00:59,324
That is our rule for today.

15
00:00:59,604 --> 00:01:04,211
The conversion runs in a sealed process, with no network at all.

16
00:01:04,661 --> 00:01:09,404
And if a page is only a scanned image, Tesseract OCR can read it,

17
00:01:09,404 --> 00:01:12,688
and marks that text with how confident it is.

18
00:01:13,138 --> 00:01:19,008
The Markdown gets its own fingerprint, and every citation is checked against it later.

19
00:01:20,292 --> 00:01:24,868
Before building anything, the platform checks the document itself.

20
00:01:25,318 --> 00:01:28,379
Six simple checks, out of hundred.

21
00:01:28,659 --> 00:01:34,532
Is the text readable? Do the tables have proper column names? Are there headings?

22
00:01:34,532 --> 00:01:39,173
Does it say its version and owner? Is anything still marked TBD?

23
00:01:39,623 --> 00:01:44,539
There is no AI here, so the same file always gets the same score.

24
00:01:44,989 --> 00:01:48,161
Our spreadsheet scores hundred. Ready.

25
00:01:48,441 --> 00:01:54,251
A raw export with no column names gets sixty, with the reason for every point lost.

26
00:01:54,701 --> 00:01:57,953
It only suggests. It never blocks.

27
00:01:59,250 --> 00:02:00,786
Now the graph.

28
00:02:01,066 --> 00:02:05,605
The document becomes one node, and each heading becomes a section.

29
00:02:06,055 --> 00:02:10,428
Each table row becomes a record, and each cell becomes a value.

30
00:02:10,878 --> 00:02:16,268
So our rule is linked to, verified by code review and automated test,

31
00:02:16,268 --> 00:02:18,690
and, on breach, blocks release.

32
00:02:19,140 --> 00:02:21,824
Three other rules also block release,

33
00:02:21,824 --> 00:02:26,032
so they meet at the same value node. That linking is free.

34
00:02:26,482 --> 00:02:30,776
Every node remembers its line, and its source fingerprint.

35
00:02:31,056 --> 00:02:34,007
No AI so far, and no cost.

36
00:02:35,292 --> 00:02:39,774
Same way, let's add four more files: the coding standard,

37
00:02:39,774 --> 00:02:45,987
the API specification, the test traceability matrix, and the outage postmortem.

38
00:02:46,437 --> 00:02:51,819
Each goes through the same steps: Markdown, quality check, structure.

39
00:02:52,269 --> 00:02:54,249
All four score hundred.

40
00:02:54,529 --> 00:02:57,386
The source check also notices that three of

41
00:02:57,386 --> 00:03:00,376
these files point to the same page-size rule.

42
00:03:00,826 --> 00:03:05,431
Now read them together, and the story is honestly quite shocking.

43
00:03:05,881 --> 00:03:10,664
In May, the test review found that patient search had no upper limit.

44
00:03:11,114 --> 00:03:14,058
In June, it was waived for the release.

45
00:03:14,338 --> 00:03:19,425
And in August, someone searched patients with a limit of one lakh records.

46
00:03:19,875 --> 00:03:22,191
The service read the whole table,

47
00:03:22,191 --> 00:03:26,471
and for forty-five minutes, sixty percent of requests failed.

48
00:03:27,792 --> 00:03:32,892
Structure tells us where things are written. To understand what they mean,

49
00:03:32,892 --> 00:03:33,925
we bring in AI.

50
00:03:34,375 --> 00:03:40,875
OpenAI or Claude reads each file, but it can only use a fixed vocabulary: eight types,

51
00:03:40,875 --> 00:03:43,974
like requirement, component and incident,

52
00:03:43,974 --> 00:03:48,660
and twelve relationships, like affects, violates and verifies.

53
00:03:49,110 --> 00:03:53,577
So it proposes: the outage affects the patient search API,

54
00:03:53,577 --> 00:03:56,734
and that API violates the page-size rule.

55
00:03:57,184 --> 00:04:00,350
Every proposal must carry an exact quote.

56
00:04:00,630 --> 00:04:05,273
If the quote is not found word for word, it is simply rejected.

57
00:04:05,723 --> 00:04:11,123
The same API, named the same way in two files, becomes one node.

58
00:04:11,573 --> 00:04:16,704
And only new or changed files go to the model, within the monthly budget.

59
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:20,687
Next, we prepare for search.

60
00:04:20,967 --> 00:04:27,739
A keyword index, BM25, is always built. It is fast, and it is free.

61
00:04:28,189 --> 00:04:33,066
If the owner switches on embeddings, each passage also gets a vector,

62
00:04:33,066 --> 00:04:37,166
so a question can match by meaning, and not just by words.

63
00:04:37,616 --> 00:04:39,312
Both rankings are combined,

64
00:04:39,312 --> 00:04:41,323
and the graph can walk two steps

65
00:04:41,323 --> 00:04:44,841
along these relationships to collect connected evidence.

66
00:04:46,125 --> 00:04:49,118
Every build is saved as a numbered draft.

67
00:04:49,398 --> 00:04:53,655
Graphify groups the nodes into themes using Leiden clustering,

68
00:04:53,655 --> 00:04:55,989
so the graph is easier to explore.

69
00:04:56,439 --> 00:05:01,476
And the draft is tested on its own: on CarePath's sixteen test questions,

70
00:05:01,476 --> 00:05:04,375
the right document comes back for fifteen.

71
00:05:05,667 --> 00:05:09,857
Finally, a person reviews the draft and publishes it.

72
00:05:10,137 --> 00:05:13,254
Only the published version answers questions.

73
00:05:13,534 --> 00:05:18,537
So let's ask: why did the service go down on the fourteenth of August?

74
00:05:18,987 --> 00:05:24,791
The answer points to the postmortem, the test-review finding, and the page-size rule.

75
00:05:25,241 --> 00:05:30,042
And every citation is checked again, live: the source is still active,

76
00:05:30,042 --> 00:05:34,293
the fingerprint has not changed, and the quote is still there.

77
00:05:34,743 --> 00:05:37,659
Restricted documents are never used.

78
00:05:37,939 --> 00:05:42,556
So, one spreadsheet became Markdown, then a small graph.

79
00:05:42,836 --> 00:05:47,020
Four files later, the graph tells the complete story:

80
00:05:47,020 --> 00:05:50,653
the rule, the gap, the waiver, and the outage.

81
00:05:51,103 --> 00:05:54,488
And every line points back to where it came from.
